title: Example - Markdown image - base64 data
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki;vocab=Markdown

`![alt](#image-tiddler.jpg "tooltip")` — TW markdown plugin syntax for an image whose source tiddler has its bytes in the `text` field (the most common case for self-contained TW wikis). The [IMAGE marker](#vocab/markdown/IMAGE) emits a `<$image source="...">` widget; TW's image widget detects the source is a tiddler with image-content type and inlines the data as a `data:image/jpeg;base64,...` URL — no network request, the image is part of the wiki.

The CommonMark mapping holds: body → `alt`, `"..."` parameter → `tooltip`. When either is absent, the source tiddler's `alt-text` field fills in. (For the full priority resolution including the smart-alt accessibility rule, see [the body-override example](#Example - Markdown image - body override).)

## 1 — `#` prefix with URL-encoded spaces

![A photograph of Motovun Jack on a stone wall](#Motovun%20Jack.jpg)

```
![A photograph of Motovun Jack on a stone wall](#Motovun%20Jack.jpg)
```

`%20` is the URL encoding for a space. After `#`-strip the engine `decodeURIComponent`s the rest so the lookup matches the `Motovun Jack.jpg` tiddler. The body becomes the rendered `alt`.

## 2 — Angle-bracket wrap for literal spaces

![A photograph of Motovun Jack on a stone wall](<#Motovun Jack.jpg>)

```
![A photograph of Motovun Jack on a stone wall](<#Motovun Jack.jpg>)
```

`<...>` accepts literal whitespace, no encoding needed. Inside the wrap, `\<` / `\>` unescape to literal angle brackets if the title needs them.

## 3 — Trailing `"tooltip"` parameter

![Motovun Jack](#Motovun Jack.jpg "A photograph of Motovun Jack on a stone wall")

```
![Motovun Jack](#Motovun Jack.jpg "A photograph of Motovun Jack on a stone wall")
```

The trailing `"..."` becomes `tooltip` (rendered as `<img title>`). Hover the image to see it.

Note: the body here is `Motovun Jack` (2 words). The source tiddler's `alt-text` field is more descriptive (≥3 words), so the ''smart-alt rule'' kicks in: the rendered `alt` comes from the source field, not from the user's 2-word body. The user's `"..."` parameter still wins for `tooltip`. See [body-override](#Example - Markdown image - body override) for a closer look.

## 4 — Empty body (decorative image)

![](<#Motovun Jack.jpg>)

```
![](<#Motovun Jack.jpg>)
```

Empty body + source tiddler with an `alt-text` field → DOM `alt` and `tooltip` both come from the field via `attr-alt-from` / `attr-tooltip-from`. Nothing for the user to write at the callsite; the source tiddler's semantic data is authoritative.

## How the widget renders

The source tiddler [Motovun Jack.jpg](#Motovun Jack.jpg) has `type: image/jpeg` and a `text` field carrying base64-encoded JPEG bytes. TW's image widget builds `src="data:image/jpeg;base64," + text` and assigns the result to `<img src>`. The browser decodes the data URL and renders the image inline — no network round-trip.

